  "query_preview": ".. Today, generally benign conditions persist as height \nrises gradually build into the region. Modest surface winds with \ndiurnal shifts are expected with clear and sunny skies for the rest \nof today. \n\nStarting tomorrow, confidence regarding the fire weather threat \nacross central and southern UT continues to increase as a deepening \nmidlevel trough progresses toward the Great Basin. A strengthening \npressure gradient amidst strong mixing favors strong winds \ntranslating to the surface out of the southwest. Chances for gusts \ngreater than 30mph range from 50-80% across most central and \nsouthern UT valleys on Saturday and 90%+ across southwest UT. \nAdditionally, chances greater than 40mph remain around 20-40% across \nmost of southern and central UT with 60%+ across southwest UT. With \npoor overnight recoveries and widespread single digit humidities \nSaturday afternoon, critical fire weather conditions will exist \nacross central and southern UT Saturday. \n\nOn both Saturday and Sunday, there is at least some potential for \nisolated scattered dry thunderstorms with attendant strong to severe \nmicrobursts. Widespread PVA amidst height falls and inverted-V \nsoundings across northern UT may favor the development of these \nstorms across northern UT Saturday afternoon. Lightning is also \npossible with these storms in the presence of some instability aloft, \npotentially yielding a more localized fire weather threat across \nnorthern UT Saturday afternoon with lightning probabilities maxing \nout around 15%. The threat shifts more toward central UT on Sunday \nas multiple shortwave perturbations traverse across the state, \nserving as ascent for these storms. There is high uncertainty \nsurrounding an initiation signal at this time for Sunday afternoon, \nthough hazards would remain similar to the storms on Saturday if \nthey do form.  \n\nOn Sunday across southern and central UT, a frontal boundary will \nslowly move east with time keeping the gusty wind threat more \nconfined to southeast UT. Critical fire weather conditions will \nremain in place across central and southern UT, though winds are \nexpected to taper off with a midlevel trough lifting north of the \narea displacing synoptic wind support from the region somewhat. \nDespite this, confidence continues to exist regarding critical fire \nweather conditions due to ample coverage of expected wind gusts \ngreater than 30mph (greater than 60% chance) and humidities in the \nsingle digits to low teens.\n\nMonday and Tuesday, however, bring a somewhat different fire weather \nsetup. While humidities may rebound somewhat into the low teens, \nthere is high confidence in widespread winds greater than 30mph \nacross central and souther UT (generally 60-90%+). Additionally, the \nsignal for gusts greater than 40mph is relatively widespread, \nranging from 40-60% and increasing somewhat with eastern extent. In \nparticular, this signal is most pronounced on Tuesday when the \nmidlevel pressure gradient tightens with the eastward progression of \na midlevel trough across the Great Basin. Lastly, there is also some \nsignal for dry thunderstorms once again on Tuesday, though exact \nlocation is extremely uncertain at this time. \n\nWednesday will bring another round of fire weather conditions to \ncentral and southern UT, though somewhat muted compared to the prior \ntwo days as a frontal boundary will begin progressing through the \nregion as the midlevel trough begins to lift away from the forecast \narea. There is some potential that near-critical fire weather \nconditions extend past Wednesday, though high uncertainty surrounds \nthis.",
